            text="""I'll never forget the typhoon that hit my city a couple of years ago. We had heard about it on the news for days, so we bought some food and water and charged all our phones the night before. When it arrived, it was much worse than I expected. The wind was so strong that our windows were shaking, and around midnight the power went out in the whole building. We just sat in the living room with a flashlight, and my daughter was really scared, so we told her stories to keep her calm. The next morning, the street outside looked like a disaster area. There were fallen trees everywhere, and some parts of the road were flooded up to my knees. I couldn't go to work for two days. What impressed me, though, was how people reacted. Neighbors who usually never talked helped each other move trees and clean up. It was a frightening experience, but it also made me realize how important it is to be prepared.""",
